pastex (2.33.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New configuration key `client_secret`, next to `client_id`. Google's
    Personal mode onboarding creates a "Web application" OAuth client, and that
    client needs its secret when a sign-in code is exchanged for tokens. The key
    is optional: a client created without a secret still works with it left out,
    and a value that is clearly not a secret is reported instead of being sent to
    Google to be refused.
  * The connection now survives instead of expiring. Pastex used Google's
    implicit flow, which returns an access token that dies after an hour and
    cannot be renewed, so every restart asked for a sign-in again. It now uses
    the authorization-code flow with PKCE and `access_type=offline`, keeps the
    refresh token in `chrome.storage.local`, and renews silently: a worker that
    Chrome stopped comes back and carries on without showing a page. The access token
    itself is still never written to storage.
  * Signing out revokes the refresh token as well, so nothing can renew a
    connection that was ended on purpose.
  * Fixed: the newline buttons did nothing on a contenteditable field, which is
    what WhatsApp Web, chat boxes and mail composers use. The text was inserted
    as one run, and HTML has no newline inside a text node, so the appended
    newline became nothing at all while the panel still said "Inserted.". Each
    line is now inserted separately with a real line break between them. Pastex
    never presses Enter, so a message on WhatsApp is not sent by inserting one.
  * The README documents the Personal mode route end to end, including why the
    secret lives in the user's own configuration file and why it is not compiled
    into the extension.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 29 Nov 2026 09:00:00 +0000

pastex (2.32.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed: a snippet typed while a sync was running could disappear. Sending a
    write invalidates the cached snapshot, and an edit that arrived in exactly
    that window had no snapshot to be applied to. It was answered as "saved"
    and queued, but the row vanished from the list and, once the flush had
    finished reading the queue, its write was not sent either.
  * An edit that arrives during a write now waits for that write to settle and
    is applied to the snapshot it leaves behind, so the row stays on screen and
    its write joins the queue.
  * Fixed: a read that started before a row was typed (the idle check, or the
    read a flush does after its writes) installed a library that had never heard
    of that row, deleting it from the panel. Such a read now puts back the rows
    Drive does not have; a row Drive does report is still described by Drive's
    answer.
  * A copy of the snapshot handed back after a flush is refused when anything
    was written since it was taken, instead of overwriting that write.
  * Fixed: two panel opens at the same moment read the library twice instead of
    sharing one read.

