pastex (2.17.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The .deb is now a well-formed Debian package, not only a valid archive:
      - it ships md5sums, so `dpkg -V pastex` can notice a corrupted or
        locally edited file; the digests are generated from the staged tree
        and cover every shipped file except the control area
      - it declares Installed-Size (390 KiB), which apt uses to size the
        install before downloading
      - it ships /usr/share/doc/pastex/changelog.Debian.gz, so
        `apt changelog pastex` shows this file instead of failing
      - it declares Multi-Arch: foreign, correct for a data-only payload
  * Builds are reproducible. Two builds of the same commit with the same CRX
    key now produce a byte-identical .deb: every timestamp comes from
    SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH (the commit time by default), the staging tree is
    touched to match, gzip keeps "-n", and dpkg-deb runs with
    --uniform-compression. Previously the two archives differed only in tar
    timestamps, which made the published checksums.txt impossible to verify
    by rebuilding.
  * debian/control carries the same Multi-Arch field, and a test keeps both
    packaging paths in step.
  * CI lints the package with `lintian --fail-on error` before publishing;
    lintian's error classes are packaging bugs, not style, and now fail the
    build instead of reaching the archive.
  * Five new tests build the package and inspect it: md5sums verifies against
    the unpacked payload, Installed-Size matches du, the changelog is the real
    project changelog, and two consecutive builds compare byte for byte.

