    machine xclip reports success, the clipboard reads back empty even after
    Tk claims the selection, and 1.10.5 therefore cancelled the paste. The
    text now arrives anyway: when the clipboard cannot be made to hold it,
    Pastex types the snippet into the target window with xdotool (through a
    temporary file, so a snippet that starts with "-" is not read as an
    option) instead of pressing Ctrl+V on an empty clipboard. The text is
    delivered exactly once either way, and only a text longer than 20 000
    characters stops the paste and says so.
  * Believe only a non-empty clipboard read. A single broken reader used to
    make a full clipboard look empty and cancel a paste that would have
    worked; xclip, xsel and wl-paste are all consulted now and an empty answer
    is accepted only when every reader agrees.
  * pastex --check explains the empty read-back and the typed delivery.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@gmail.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:54:00 +0700

pastex (1.10.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Verify the copy before pasting. xclip, xsel and wl-copy exit 0 once they
    have handed the text to the process they fork, so a copy whose input was
    lost still reports success and then serves an empty clipboard: the log of
    the Cinnamon session showed "copy: ok=True tool=xclip (9 ms)" while
    pastex --check read the clipboard back as empty. Ctrl+V would then paste
    nothing at all. The clipboard is now read back (a second time, because a
    fresh owner can answer late) before Ctrl+V is sent.
  * Claim the selection with Tk when the tool lost it. If the clipboard does
    not hold the text, Pastex sets it through Tk itself -- no subprocess can
    lose that -- verifies again, and only then sends Ctrl+V.
  * Say so instead of pasting nothing. When no clipboard holds the text the
    paste is cancelled, the window is restored and the status reports that
    nothing was pasted, instead of the "the text is on the clipboard" hint.
  * pastex --check explains an empty read-back the same way.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@gmail.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:53:00 +0700

pastex (1.10.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Decide the paste from the input focus instead of the window state.
    Muffin on a Cinnamon X11 session (wm=Mutter (Muffin)) reported
    state=normal for the whole 900 ms budget even though the window had been
    iconified, and the client window stays IsViewable while a reparenting WM
    unmaps the frame around it, so xwininfo could not answer either. Both
    checks therefore refused to send Ctrl+V on a machine where the minimize
    worked. What the paste actually needs is the focus back on the target
    application, so the active window is now read with xdotool every 120 ms
    (a frame that still belongs to Pastex is recognised through its WM_CLASS)
    and that verdict decides the paste. WM_STATE and xwininfo remain the
    fallback when xdotool cannot answer.
  * Treat a viewable client window as "cannot tell" rather than "still on
    screen", and log the focus verdict, the mechanism that decided the paste
    and the observed focus in the give-up line.
  * Do not report an empty clipboard read-back as a mismatch: xclip forks the
    owner that takes the selection over, so pastex --check now re-reads once
    before judging and explains an empty clipboard instead.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@gmail.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:52:00 +0700

pastex (1.10.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Make the paste work on a machine whose window manager ignores the
    minimize request. 1.10.2 restored the window but never sent Ctrl+V,
    because iconify() did nothing at all: the state stayed "normal" for the
    whole 600 ms budget. The hide step now unmaximizes first (Tk/X11 drops
    iconify() for a zoomed window and puts the zoom back on restore), then, if
    the state still says normal, asks the window manager to minimize through
    xdotool's EWMH request, and finally asks the X server itself (xwininfo)
    whether the window is really unmapped -- a window manager that lags or
    skips WM_STATE then no longer costs the paste. The budget is 900 ms and
    the give-up line logs the Tk state, the X verdict, whether the WM was
    asked and whether the window was zoomed.
  * Name the window manager. The ready: line logs the window manager (or
    "none"), XDG_SESSION_TYPE and XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP, and pastex --check
    prints the same, because without a window manager nothing can minimize
    the window and automatic paste is not available at all.
  * Stop a picker paste from waiting for the main window. A popup paste
    destroys the popup and leaves the main window visible on purpose, but the
    hide check read the main window's state and gave up after 900 ms without
    sending Ctrl+V. The check skips the state for a popup paste.
  * Refresh the tree without inotify. When inotify cannot be opened the
    watcher falls back to polling the folders every 2 s instead of switching
    to manual refresh only, and the log names the errno.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@gmail.com>  Tue, 17 Nov 2026 20:40:00 +0700

pastex (1.10.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Never leave the window minimized, and stop skipping Ctrl+V on a slow
    minimize. The hide check gave up after 300 ms, but Cinnamon animates the
    minimize for a few hundred milliseconds; when the window finished hiding
    just after the check, Pastex had already decided "never hidden", sent no
    Ctrl+V and left the window minimized. The check now allows 600 ms.
  * Restore the window instead of giving up on it. The branch that concluded
    "the window never hid" did not deiconify anything, so a minimize that
    completed a moment later was permanent. It now asks for the window back
    and logs the observed state.
  * Verify the restore. deiconify() is dropped when it arrives while the
    minimize animation is still running, which also left the window minimized
    for good. The state is now re-read every 60 ms for up to 1.2 s and the
    request is repeated until the window is really back, in every path
    (success, failure, watchdog deadline and the give-up case).
  * Do not overwrite a running paste with the manual hint. When the <Unmap>
    signal released Ctrl+V before the copy worker reported back, the copy
    result replaced the status with "press Ctrl+V" even though the sender was
    already running; a _paste_started flag keeps that path quiet.
  * Seven new tests and three new mutation checks cover the restore path.

